everyone uh today i'll be talking about  a very detailed survey that we did here  at healthtrack  rx about the bacterial and viral  co-infections  of sarskoff ii and uh to our knowledge  this is the largest study or largest  such study  into the co-infection rates of this  noble coronavirus  just close this slide here so the sars  column  ii which is a latest  coronavirus member the coronavirus  family  uh named as such due to the  characteristic distribution  of the spike protein on the viral  surface  is responsible for causing from mild  to fatal respiratory illnesses  in humans and when we compare the  epidemiology of this viral disease  with the other known respiratory  pathogens we find the infectivity of  this virus  is somewhere between influenza  and sars cob one which was responsible  for the 2002  stars pandemic and  since the first official declaration  of this viral outbreak at the end of  2019 the global pandemic has infected  over 29 million people the numbers that  i have here  are are out of date now  and within the united states there are  over 6.5 million confirmed cases  of the viral infection and more than 194  000  deaths and what we have seen  in recent memory the most unprecedented  mobilization of public health resources  in our fight against  this virus which has had a  great impact on our economic  and social behavior  and quite literally our knowledge of how  this virus works and the covert 19  disease that it causes our knowledge  of this virus is increasing  every day however what we find is that  the co-infections or the bacterial and  viral co-infections of sarskoff do  that's an area where our understanding  is  still quite limited now what we know  from our  experience with the other respiratory  viruses  is that the concurrent or secondary  infections  of these viruses with other bacteria  lead to an increased morbidity and  mortality  in patients and in fact the  general consensus now is that the  all the influenza pandemics of the last  century  the leading cause of mortality in those  patients were the  secondary or infections with  bacterial pathogens and this is due to  the fact that these respiratory  pathogens  both the bacteria and the viruses have  co-evolved in a manner to take advantage  of the preceding infection  so here at healthtrack rx co-infections  of respiratory pathogens is an area that  we're all  always interested in and in 2019 we had  published  us a very detailed analysis  of co-infections in  respiratory viral pathogens influenza  rsv  and others and uh the present data set  like i said uh is the largest study that  we know of  it comprised of 50 419  individual samples that were tested  between mid-march  and the beginning of august this year  and the  samples were concurrently tested for  cough ii using the tacpath coca 19 kit  from thermo fisher  and all the other pathogens both  bacterial and viral  uh respiratory pathogens were tested  on the nanofluidic open array platform  also from thermo fisher  and what we found was that the sars cost  2  infection rate at 8.4 percent  was very similar to the  all the other viral pathogens put  together at 8.2 percent  uh we found in the overall population  a bacterial infection rate of around  33 now when we do a demographic  distribution  of the sars of two infections uh  based on gender we find that the female  and male  ratio was quite similar to the overall  population set that we had  with nearly 56 females and 44  males and when we distribute the same  according to age uh what we found was  quite interesting  is that overall the younger population  that is between 20 to 50 years of age  comprised both male and female comprised  a  higher proportion as opposed to the  older population  that is 55 or 60 years and  higher  when we look at the types of  co-infections  what we find is that the distribution of  the bacterial co-infections  in both the stars of two positive and  stars of two negative population  is quite similar the numbers are you  know statistically speaking  are significantly lower in the south to  positive  population but you know still comparable  at 33 and 35 percent  however this difference is more  pronounced  in the viral co-infections where in the  sars of true positive population  the viral co-infection rates are much  lower as compared to the source of two  negative  population and  when we go actually go into the  different types of co-infections with  the bacterial co-infections  we find the usual bacterial  pathogens namely streptococcus  pneumoniae stuff  staphorious hemophilus influenza that  are represented as the  major or infecting agents  klebsiella pneumonia also but to a lower  level as compared to these other  pathogens  but what we see is that the bacterial  uh infection rates or co-infection rates  in the source  of the positive population are lower as  opposed to the sarska of two negative  population with the exception of staph  aureus  wherein the infection rates are  statistically significant  and higher in the sars of two  positive now um with the  informal discussions with other labs we  find that this is not a unique  uh observation at our end and the other  labs are  also seeing this and we find this quite  interesting and this is something that  we are looking at  much more closely now is to basically  figure out  what percentage of this is represented  by the methicillin resistant staph  aureus or mrsa  because that has a very significant  impact on the morbidity and mortality  that is associated with  respiratory infections now  we also found in the general population  we found  less than 0.1 percent infection rate of  chlamydophila pneumoniae microplasma  pneumonia and bordetella pertussis  and no co-infections with those  pathogens and that is why that's not  represented here  when we look at the viral co-infections  the majority of the pathogens as you  will see here  uh represent a less than one percent  prevalence rate and that could very well  be due to the fact  that when we started collecting this  data it was towards the end of march  and that coincides with the end  of the respiratory pathogen season or  the flu season here in the united states  and again what we see  here with the blue bars the stars of two  positive  population the co-infection rates of  these viruses  are lower as opposed to the  stars of two negative population with  the exception  of rsv and  again i apologize for some confusion  here the hsv here  is a combination of hsv one and two and  the hsv at the  end here is hsb3  or the varicella zoster virus and these  are the only two viruses  wherein we see a higher co-infection  in the sarskoff to positive population  now  the other exception that we saw was ebv  the epstein-barr virus or hsb4  and the rhinoviruses where we see  a higher than one percent prevalence  rate in the general population  a significantly higher ebv co-infection  rate was also seen  in the sars of true population and if  you go through the latest literature  what you find  is that these ebv co-infection or higher  evv co-infection rates in saarskoff two  positive patients have been reported  from other sources  also  when we look at the  age distribution of these co-infections  put together both  bacterial and viral in the sense of true  positive population what we find  is that the older population 60  plus years they are more susceptible to  these co-infections  and again  if you would recall the age distribution  of the source of to positive population  was more towards the younger side  however the co-infections are higher  in the older population and  this could very well contribute to the  poor prognosis of the coca-19 disease  that we observe in the older population  and again what we are doing right now  since as i've mentioned before  most of this study was done towards the  end of the flu season so what we are  doing  right now is that we are actively  collecting data now that fall is upon us  and  and winter is coming quite literally uh  that we will be looking at these four  infections  in much more detail and uh  will be presenting a follow-up of the  study towards the end of the  flu season in 2021 so to  summarize what we see is that the source  of true population  positive population displays a  significant co-infection  of both bacterial and viral  pathogens and generally speaking the  varico infections in the source of true  positive population  were found to be significantly lower  as opposed to the sars of two negative  population and within the viral  co-infections we find  that there the ebv or the epstein-barr  virus  co-infections are detected at a  significantly higher rate  in the sars of two positive population  when compared to the  all other respiratory viral pathogens  and amongst the bacterial pathogens in  the source of positive population we  find  a significantly higher co-infection rate  of staph aureus  and when distributed against age  what we find is that put together the  viral and bacterial co-infections  are observed at a higher rate in the  older population  that is 60 years of age and higher  as opposed to the all other age groups  and what we believe is that the data  presented here in the follow-up study  that we are doing right now  will allow for a better clinical  decision  to be made with respect to you know the  antimicrobial therapy that can be  provided to the infected subjects  for a you know for better clinical  treatment  and uh that i would like to  acknowledge the infectious disease team  here at healthtrack rx  uh namely my co-authors on this study dr  j  reddy the vp of lab operations and dr  john granger  our medical director and all the other  scientists  and laboratory personnel working here at  infectious disease in health track rx  and with that i'll take any of your  questions thank you
 

